Major Flora of Pandora

Habitat (Location)
 (1) Subterranean: Entirely or primarily underground.
 (2) Terrestrial: Rooted in soil; primarily above-ground.
 (3) Epiphytic: Growing on tree trunks; often attached via woody vines.
 (4) Arboreal: Growing on/under tree limbs; uses hanging or herbaceous vines.

Strategy (Engagement)
 (1) Passive/Opportunistic: Relies on high-traffic locations and chance.
 (2) Alluring/Baiting: Uses lures (scent, visual, auditory) to attract prey.
 (3) Insidious/Ambush: Camouflaged and stationary until the strike.
 (4) Proactive/Stalking: Actively moves appendages to close the gap upon detection.

Mechanism (The Capture)
 [P] Passive (Low Energy)
   (1) Sequestrators: Uses gravity, pits, or adhesives; no active movement.
   (2) Reflexive-Closers: Uses stored pressure to snap "jaws" or doors shut.
   (3) Endophytic: Parasitic; grows seeds/roots inside a living host.
   (4) Pheromonal/Narcotic: Uses chemical "hacking" to disable or control prey.
 [A] Active (High Energy)
   (1) Brachial-Graspers: Predatory tentacles that actively reach, grab, and pull.
   (2) Thigmotropic-Ensnarers: Reactive "kill zones" that bind/tangle upon touch.
   (3) Traction-Tethers: Fires/drops a line and mechanically reels the prey in.
   (4) Pharyngeal-Envelopers: Muscular "mouths" that swallow and transport via a tube.

Processing (The Outcome)
 (1) Accelerated Proteolysis: Rapid acid digestion (1–2 weeks).
 (2) Sustained Bio-Maintenance: Keeps prey alive/fresh during slow consumption.
 (3) Hematic/Sap-Siphoning: Drains fluids/marrow; leaves a husk.
 (4) Necro-Fertilization: Kills prey; feeds on the resulting decay/compost.
 (5) Gestational Vectoring: Prey is used as a living nursery for seeds/spores.
 (6) Physiological Harvesting: "Milks" the host for overproduced nutrients.
 (7) Symbiotic Stewardship: Employs the animal as a defender/gatherer.


Specials
 (A) Ballistic Projectiles (Poison Darts): The plant features pressurized "blowguns" or spring-loaded spines. These fire toxin-tipped needles to cause localized or systemic paralysis from a distance.
 (B) Atmospheric Aerosols (Gas Clouds): The plant releases a heavy-than-air cloud of spores or chemicals. These can act as a sedative, a paralytic, or even a corrosive "pre-digestive" mist that softens the prey's skin.
 (C) Dermal Toxins (Contact Poison): A coating on the leaves, vines, or trunk that causes immediate reaction upon touch—ranging from agonizing "nerve-fire" (to keep the prey from struggling) to rapid necrosis.
 (D) Alkaloid Incentives (Addictive Sap): A high-sugar sap laced with addictive compounds. Once the prey tastes it, they will ignore their survival instincts to keep feeding, essentially "self-harvesting."
 (E) Bioluminescent Lures: Specialized "light-bulbs" that mimic the mating signals of insects or the glowing eyes of smaller prey to pull victims into a specific kill zone at night.
 (F) Acoustic Mimicry: Hollow chambers that use wind or internal gas-release to mimic the sounds of water, distressed animals, or even the "calls" of the prey's own species.
 (G) Thermal Radiators: The plant can rapidly increase its core temperature. This is used to lure cold-blooded prey seeking warmth
 (H) Structural Coloration
 (I) Neuro-Behavioral Hijacking: When the plant utilizes Gestational Vectoring, the implanted seed doesn't just eat the host; it releases psychoactive alkaloids into the host's nervous system. This creates a "climbing urge." The host is chemically driven to find a high-traffic tree trunk and expire there, often "hugging" the tree in its final moments.

Type 1 (Pit Trap)
 Overview: The Pit Trap is basically a hole in the ground with a false a trapdoor on the surface. The plant waits for prey to walk over the trapdoor, and then opens to let the prey fall in. Within the body of the plant there are normally tendrils that will restrain the prey, and others that will penetrate every orifice. This type of plant will keep its prey hydrated, breathing, and alive as long as possible as it siphons nutrients (First seminal fluid, vaginal fluid, milk, urine, excrement. Then Blood, Hemolymph). When the prey can no longer produce nutrients in this manner, it is digested.
 Habitat: Subterranean
 Strategy: Passive/Opportunistic
 Mechanism: Sequestrators
 Processing: Hematic/Sap-Siphoning + Sustained Bio-Maintenance
 Specials: None
 Variants: Groundmouth, Subterranean Pitcher Plant

Type 2 (Sinkhole)
 Overview: The Sinkhole is basically a large subterranean cavity with a concave pit on the surface. On the surface the pit will more than likely be partly filled with water and the sides of the pit with a type of sap that quickly adheres to flesh and then forms a nearly frictionless coating. This sap is also a potent narcotic that causes disorientation and loss of muscle control in many creatures. When a creature tries to get to the drinking water within the sinkhole they will usually fall within and slide to the bottom, whereupon a sphincter like orifice will open and drop the prey into the subterranean cavity. Within the cavity the sap hardens to form a protective shell that will preserve the prey until digestion is needed.
Habitat: Subterranean
Strategy: Passive/Opportunistic
Mechanism: Sequestrators
Processing: Sustained Bio-Maintenance
Specials: None
Variants: Meat Pit

Type 3 (Groundflower)
 Overview: The Groundflower is a large leafy plant that normally resembles a huge flower of some sort but coming out of the ground and facing the sky. Below this is a large, barely visible bulb from which dozens of long tentacle-vines stretch out in different directions. The flower part of this plant emits a scent that attracts prey to investigate, and the tentacle-vines restrain those who come within their grasp, to be deposited into a hidden "mouth" within the flower itself. Depending on the needs of the Groundflower it might penetrate every orifice to siphons nutrients (First seminal fluid, vaginal fluid, milk, urine, excrement), and then be digested or may have a large seed implanted within and released.
Habitat: Subterranean
Strategy: Alluring/Baiting + Proactive/Stalking
Mechanism: Brachial-Graspers
Processing: Sustained Bio-Maintenance + Physiological Harvesting + Gestational Vectoring
Specials: Dermal Toxins
Variants: ?

Type 4 (Meadow-Maw)
 Overview: Almost nothing can be seen of this plant above the ground other than a small field of flowers and a small fuzzy mound that is actually a sphincter-like opening to the main body below ground. From this body hundreds of short tentacle-vines piece upwards to emerge above ground as flowers. These tentacle-vines are coated with a paralytic toxin and will almost instantly wrap around and creature that touches them. Shortly after that long, thick tentacle-vines will emerge from the sphincter to drag the prey into the opening. Within the plants body the prey will be penetrated through every orifice to have its fluids siphoned (First seminal fluid, vaginal fluid, milk, urine, excrement), and eventually be digested.
Habitat: Subterranean
Strategy: Insidious/Ambush
Mechanism: Thigmotropic-Ensnarers + Brachial-Graspers
Processing: Physiological Harvesting + Sustained Bio-Maintenance
Specials: Dermal Toxins
Variants: Milkweed, Mud Lotus

Type 5 (Maw-Pillar)
 Overview: This plant resembles a short, thick palm tree with a trunk around 8–12 feet tall and fronds that reach up another 4-6 feet & out 8-12 feet. A number of large coconut like fruits that look & smell delicious hang down from the fronds close to the trunk. The "trunk" is actually a series of vertical, muscular folds that remain tightly closed. Once the prey is within reach, the "trunk" splits open down its entire length, revealing a hollow, ribbed interior lined with thousands of small, hook-like Thigmotropic-Ensnarers. The plant "folds" around the prey, sealing them inside the pillar.
Habitat: Terrestrial
Strategy: Alluring/Baiting
Mechanism: Thigmotropic-Ensnarers + Pharyngeal-Envelopers
Processing: Sustained Bio-Maintenance + Hematic/Sap-Siphoning
Specials: None
Variants: Pillar-Vine, The Iron Maiden Fern.

Type 6 (Death-Lily)
 Overview: Rising from the ground up to 20ft these large plants look like giant exotic flowers. These plants have a high degree of mobility and will use that mobility to launch an aimed vine-tentacle attack that will wrap around the target and pull them towards the flowers head, where the maw is located. Once the prey is close enough to the maw a number of smaller tentacle-vines will emerge to restrain the prey and help pull it into the maw, whereupon it will be 'swallowed, transported through via a tube, and deposited in the stomach. Within the stomach one of several things may happen depending on the variant of the plant and its nutritional needs, but in all cases the prey is slowly digested in the end.
Habitat: Terrestrial
Strategy: Insidious/Ambush
Mechanism: Brachial-Graspers + Traction-Tethers + Pharyngeal-Envelopers
Processing: Physiological Harvesting + Hematic/Sap-Siphoning + Sustained Bio-Maintenance
Specials: Atmospheric Aerosols
Variants: The Snap-Lily

Type 7 (Doom-Blossom)
Overview: This plant sits on the jungle floor, appearing as an oversized, exotic flower with thick, succulent petals. It uses a combination of Alluring Scent and Visual Lure (bright colors) to draw prey into the center of the petals. Once the weight is detected, the petals (which are actually powerful Reflexive-Closers) snap shut. Many variants have inner "hairs" or "teeth" that interlock, making escape impossible.
Habitat: Terrestrial
Strategy: Alluring/Baiting
Mechanism: Reflexive-Closers
Processing: Accelerated Proteolysis or Sustained Bio-Maintenance
Specials: Gas Clouds
Variants: ?

Type 8: (Bulbous Snatcher)
Overview: These plants feature a large, heavy "pod" or "bulb" that can be either ground-based or hanging. The pod has a singular, sphincter-like opening (the "Mouth"). It uses long, specialized vines to "snatch" prey and stuff them inside the bulb. Once inside, the Pharyngeal-Enveloper mechanics take over, and the prey is sealed in a fluid-filled chamber.
Habitat: Terrestrial or Arboreal
Strategy: Insidious/Ambush
Mechanism: Pharyngeal-Envelopers + Brachial-Graspers
Processing: Sustained Bio-Maintenance + Gestational Vectoring
Specials: Contact Poison (on the interior walls of the pod)
Variants: ?

Type 9: (Whipping Liana)
Overview: This is the most active terrestrial hunter. It doesn't look like a "trap" at all—it looks like a cluster of thick, snake-like vines. It uses Proactive/Stalking behavior to lash out at anything that enters its radius. These vines are Brachial-Graspers; they don't just trip you, they lift, constrict, and actively manipulate the prey, often pulling them toward a central "nest" or a mouth hidden in the foliage.
Habitat: Epiphytic or Arboreal
Strategy: Proactive/Stalking
Mechanism: Brachial-Graspers
Processing: Physiological Harvesting + Hematic/Sap-Siphoning
Specials: Addictive Sap
Variants: ?

Type 10 (The Strangler-Liana)
Overview: These plants consist of a massive network of woody and herbaceous vines that wrap around a host tree. Unlike a normal vine, the Strangler-Liana is highly sensitive to heat and vibration. It doesn't wait for you to touch it; it "creeps" toward resting prey. It often leaves "loops" hanging off the trunk that look like natural swings or grips. When weight is applied, or when the plant senses a body nearby, the vines cinch tight. The plant then uses microscopic thorns to anchor into the skin, preventing the prey from sliding down while it begins its work.
Habitat: Epiphytic
Strategy: Proactive/Stalking
Mechanism: Brachial-Graspers + Thigmotropic-Ensnarers
Processing: Hematic/Sap-Siphoning + Gestational Vectoring
Specials: Contact Poison
Variants: Snake-Vine, The Hangman's Noose

Type 11 (Burl-Maw)
 Overview: This plant is the ultimate ambush predator of the jungle's vertical layer. It camouflages itself perfectly as a massive, woody burl or growth on the side of a tropical hardwood tree. It is nearly impossible to distinguish from the host tree's bark until it is too late. The "growth" is actually a thick, protective shell that splits open into a vertical, fleshy maw. Once active, the plant uses several short, powerful Brachial-Graspers hidden within the "lips" of the burl to snatch prey passing by or resting against the trunk. It is known for its extreme "grip" and its tendency to use the host's elevation to keep prey safe from ground scavengers.
Habitat: Epiphytic
Strategy: Insidious/Ambush + Proactive/Stalking
Mechanism: Brachial-Graspers + Pharyngeal-Envelopers
Processing: Sustained Bio-Maintenance + Gestational Vectoring
Specials: Structural Coloration, Neuro-Behavioral Hijacking
Variants: Bark-Eye, The Wooden Womb, Tumor-Vine.

Type 12 (Rapture-Vine)
 Overview: This predator is a complex, multi-layered organism that mimics a harmless network of ivy or creeping lianas. It is almost entirely Epiphytic, using a host tree as a structural skeleton. The "outer layer" consists of fine, flower-bearing tendrils that leak Alkaloid Incentives (a sweet, addictive nectar). When a creature pauses to feed or rest, the Insidious nature of the plant reveals itself: the "inner layer" of high-strength Brachial-Graspers cinches tight. Once the prey is pinned against the trunk, the plant uses Dermal Toxins to induce a state of intense Euphoria, effectively "drugging" the victim into compliance while it begins the harvesting and siphoning process.
Habitat: Epiphytic
Strategy: Insidious/Ambush + Proactive/Stalking
Mechanism: Thigmotropic-Ensnarers + Brachial-Graspers
Processing: Hematic/Sap-Siphoning + Physiological Harvesting + Gestational Vectoring
Specials: Dermal Toxins (Euphoria) + Alkaloid Incentives (Addictive Nectar)
Variants: The Velvet Strangler, Nirvana-Creeper, Lover’s Noose.

Type 13 (Hive-Hollow)
 Overview: This plant appears as a massive, bulbous, woody growth on a tree trunk, riddled with smooth-walled tunnels that lead to a central chamber. Within the central chamber it secrets a pool of addictive fluid and uses Pheromonal/Narcotic spores to induce a state of domesticity in creatures. These animals become "defenders," attacking anything that threatens the plant and bring the plant additional food sources. However, the plant is playing a long game: it also milks them on a regular basis and eventually uses Neuro-Behavioral Hijacking to carry gestational seeds out into the jungle.
Habitat: Epiphytic
Strategy: Alluring/Baiting
Mechanism: Pheromonal/Narcotic
Processing: Physiological Harvesting + Gestational Vectoring + Symbiotic Stewardship
Specials: Alkaloid Incentives, Neuro-Behavioral Hijacking
Variants: Larder-Lump, Guest-Gallow

Type 14 (Gallows-Pitcher)
 Overview: This plant hangs from the underside of high branches by a long, high-tensile woody vine. It resembles a massive, colorful tropical fruit or a bell-shaped flower. The interior is filled with a cocktail of Alkaloid Incentives (sweet, intoxicating nectar) and digestive enzymes. Unlike terrestrial pitchers, the Gallows-Pitcher features a heavy, muscular "lid" or "hood." Once a creature enters the pitcher to feed, the plant uses Reflexive-Closers to snap the lid shut and cinch it tight. The interior walls then secrete a rapid-acting Toxin to stun the prey before they can puncture the pitcher walls.
Habitat: Arboreal
Strategy: Alluring/Baiting
Mechanism: Sequestrators + Reflexive-Closers
Processing: Accelerated Proteolysis
Specials: Alkaloid Incentives + Toxin
Variants: The Hanging Stomach, Bell-Trap, Sky-Sack.

Type 15 (Canopy-Caster)
 Overview: This plant appears as a cluster of vibrant, dew-covered vines hanging from a branch. It uses Structural Coloration to shimmer like harmless sunlight hitting water or precious gems, drawing curious prey closer. Unlike its smaller Earth cousins, the Plumbicare is a proactive hunter. When it senses movement below, it "casts" its Traction-Tether vines downward with whip-like speed. These vines are coated in an incredibly strong adhesive and feature Brachial-Grasper tips that wrap around the prey. Once a solid connection is made, the plant winches the prey upward into its central mass.
Habitat: Arboreal
Strategy: Insidious/Ambush + Proactive/Stalking
Mechanism: Brachial-Graspers + Traction-Tethers
Processing: Accelerated Proteolysis (Highly Accelerated)
Specials: Structural Coloration
Variants: Winch-Weed, The Jewel-Vine, Sky-Sundew

Type 16 (The Canopy-Angler)
 Overview: This plant grows on the underside of large tree limbs. It looks like a massive, colorful tropical flower or a heavy, hanging fruit (like a jackfruit). It dangles a long, thin, vine-like "lure" that drips a sweet-smelling, glowing nectar onto the forest floor. When a creature investigates the nectar or grabs the "vine," the plant winches the prey upward at high speed into a hanging, sack-like "stomach." The sack then seals at the top, trapping the prey in a gravity-assisted digestive bag.
Habitat: Arboreal
Strategy: Alluring/Baiting
Mechanism: Traction-Tethers + Pharyngeal-Envelopers
Processing: Sustained Bio-Maintenance + Accelerated Proteolysis
Specials: 
Variants: Wart Trap, Sky-Sack, Gallows-Fruit

Type 17 (Sky-Medusa)
 Overview: This plant features a central, fleshy hub (the "stomach-core") from which dozens of long, highly mobile tentacles radiate. While it can be found on the forest floor, it is most dangerous when Arboreal, hanging from a branch like a living chandelier. The tentacles are dual-purpose: some act as Thigmotropic-Ensnarers (sensitive triggers) while the thicker ones are powerful Brachial-Graspers. It uses a faint, bioluminescent glow or sweet-smelling pheromones as a lure. Once prey is snared, the tentacles pull the victim into the central core, where the skin is immediately breached to begin siphoning fluids.
Habitat: Primarily Arboreal but could be anywhere
Strategy: Alluring/Baiting + Proactive/Stalking
Mechanism: Brachial-Graspers + Thigmotropic-Ensnarers
Processing: Hematic/Sap-Siphoning + Accelerated Proteolysis
Specials: Dermal Toxins
Variants: Canopy-Squid, Tree-Anemone, The Hanging Medusa

Type 18 (Ballistic Reeler)
 Overview: This is a specialized, high-velocity predator that dominates the canopy. It features a series of bulbous, pressurized pods. Inside each pod is a biomineralized, bone-hard "dart" attached to a high-tensile, elastic vine. Using a rapid release of hydraulic pressure, the plant can fire these darts with enough force to penetrate feathers, hide, or light armor. The barbs ensure the prey cannot shake the dart loose, while the "winch" (a specialized, rapidly contracting vine-tissue) drags the stunned or paralyzed victim back to the plant's central digestive mass.
Habitat: Arboreal
Strategy: Insidious/Ambush
Mechanism: Traction-Tethers
Processing: Hematic/Sap-Siphoning, Accelerated Proteolysis, or Necro-Fertilization
Specials: Ballistic Projectiles
Variants: Harpoon Plant

Type 19 (Aegis-Vine)
 Overview: Overview: This is a Structural Symbiote that acts as a secondary, biological skin. It begins as a fine, moss-like coating that eventually weaves itself into a dense, woody lattice over the host's entire body. Instead of draining the host to death, it performs Sustained Bio-Maintenance, filtering toxins from the host's blood and even secreting healing resins into wounds. In exchange, the plant feeds on the host's excess glucose and uses the host's mobility to find optimal sunlight. It essentially turns the host into a "Living Tank," providing natural armor and potentially even camouflaging them with the environment.
Habitat: Anywhere
Strategy: Insidious/Ambush -> Symbiotic/Defensive
Mechanism: Thigmotropic-Ensnarers
Processing: Hematic/Sap-Siphoning + Sustained Bio-Maintenance + Symbiotic Stewardship
Specials: Structural Coloration + Regenerative Resins
Variants: Sentinel-Moss

Type 20 (Hollow-Sprout)
 Overview: This plant represents the "Biological End-Game." It is a small, succulent-looking flower or fruit that is highly edible and delicious (Alkaloid Incentives). However, the seeds are armored and designed to survive digestion. Once inside the host's digestive tract, they latch onto the intestinal wall and begin to grow. Instead of killing the host quickly, the plant performs Physiological Harvesting, feeding off the host. Eventually, the plant "blooms" by sending shoots out through the host’s orifices or skin, slowly consuming the soft tissues from within until the host is hollowed out and replaced by the plant's woody structure.
Habitat: Anywhere
Strategy: Alluring/Baiting
Mechanism: Endophytic
Processing: Hematic/Sap-Siphoning + Necro-Fertilization
Specials: Alkaloid Incentives, Neuro-Behavioral Hijacking
Variants: Gut-Lily, Hollow-Bloom, Parasitic Orchard

